        // Arduino PC page
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.Title"] = "PC control with Arduino",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.Subtitle"] = "Personal project to control a computer using Arduino microcontrollers and Unity",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.IntroArduino.Title"] = "What is Arduino?",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.IntroArduino.Description"] = "Arduino is an open-source electronics prototyping platform based on flexible, easy-to-use hardware and software. I discovered it in technology class at the end of middle school, where we programmed with MBlock, a Scratch-based tool adapted for Arduino. To go further, I made this project to introduce myself to electronics and embedded programming in C/C++ (the programming language used with the Arduino IDE).",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.IntroUnity.Title"] = "The role of Unity",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.IntroUnity.Description"] = "I also wanted to get started with software development. To begin with something simple, I decided to learn how to use Unity, a very powerful game engine, but here I used it as a software interface (in C#). The program running in the background on the PC listens to the serial port and receives the data sent by the Arduino to trigger specific actions on Windows, such as opening applications or simulating keyboard input.",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.Project.Title"] = "The project",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.Project.Description"] = "The initial idea was to design a physical control box for my computer. However, my Arduino Uno board alone could not accommodate all my components and could not emulate a keyboard and a mouse. So I made two enclosures (one with an Arduino Uno and the other with an Arduino Leonardo for keyboard/mouse emulation). These two boxes are only connected to the PC via a USB cable; to make them communicate, I decided to create a Unity application as an intermediary. Below you can see several images of the completion of this full project combining electronics and software. This project taught me a lot about C/C++, infrared control, the basics of C# and Unity, and electronics.",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.ViewGithub"] = "View the GitHub project",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.ViewYoutube"] = "Watch the YouTube video",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.Carousel.Image1Alt"] = "Enclosure number 1",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.Carousel.Image1Cap"] = "Arduino Leonardo enclosure with an LCD screen, infrared receiver, and buttons",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.Carousel.Image2Alt"] = "Enclosure number 2",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.Carousel.Image2Cap"] = "Arduino Uno enclosure with buttons, joystick, and digital display",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.Carousel.Image3Alt"] = "Unity software interface",
        ["Portfolio.ArduinoPc.Carousel.Image3Cap"] = "The Unity interface used as a relay for the Arduinos",

        // Death Ire page
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.Title"] = "My first real video game: Death Ire",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.Subtitle"] = "Introduction to C# and Unity by creating a complete mobile game",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.Intro.Title"] = "Why this project?",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.Intro.Description"] = "After exploring Arduino and even creating a small Unity application for the Arduino project, I wanted to deepen my knowledge of C#, because I really enjoyed building that application. I decided to learn Unity for what it was designed for: making video games.",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.GameExpl.Title"] = "The game",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.GameExpl.Description"] = "Death Ire is a challenging platformer with different mechanics: teleportation using a bow, combat, climbing, and sliding. The character would travel through different worlds to unlock these new mechanics. The game also features collecting pets, obtained by solving puzzles.",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.GameDesign.Title"] = "Game design & menus",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.GameDesign.Description"] = "I became very interested in game design, level design, and menu design, trying to be original and as intuitive as possible.",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.GameDesign.Carousel.Item1Alt"] = "Main menu preview",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.GameDesign.Carousel.Item2Alt"] = "Designing a room in the Unity engine",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.GameDesign.Carousel.Item2Cap"] = "Room design",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.GameDesign.Carousel.Item3Alt"] = "Unity game shop",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.GameDesign.Carousel.Item3Cap"] = "In-game shop for skins",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.LevelGen.Title"] = "Level generation",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.LevelGen.Description"] = "One of Death Ire's technical challenges was creating random yet reproducible levels through saving. When starting a new game, the game generates all levels (platform positions and save points) randomly while trying to reach a similar difficulty level.",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.Conclusion.Title"] = "Conclusion",
        ["Portfolio.DeathIre.Conclusion.Description"] = "Ultimately, my game Death Ire allowed me to build solid foundations in algorithms, C#, logical code structuring, as well as physics handling, UI, animation creation, and save systems with Unity. These skills were crucial for what came next: my degree and my professional career in .NET development. You can see on the right a small preview of the movement script without comments, but in reality the game includes more than 30 different scripts and a total of more than ten thousand lines of code, even though I never finished the game.",

        // BAC STI2D page
        ["Portfolio.BacSti2d.Title"] = "STI2D High School Diploma project: Remote-controlled kart",
        ["Portfolio.BacSti2d.Subtitle"] = "Design and control of a motorized kart",
        ["Portfolio.BacSti2d.Intro.Title"] = "What is this project?",
        ["Portfolio.BacSti2d.Intro.Description"] = "This project was completed at the end of my final year in STI2D for the high school diploma exam. The goal was to transform a standard electric kart into a kart that could be controlled remotely using a mobile phone and its gyroscope. This involved electronics with Arduino, mechanics, and software development. It was my first major team project. My two teammates, specialized in ITEC (Technological Innovation and Eco-Design), were responsible for installing the linear actuator in place of the original steering system and ensuring the kart could turn properly. I was assigned to the entire IT and electrical part of the project.",
        ["Portfolio.BacSti2d.System.Title"] = "System and architecture",
        ["Portfolio.BacSti2d.System.Description"] = "I designed the overall system architecture. I used an Arduino Uno as the microcontroller and developed all the embedded logic in C++, which consisted of receiving movement data for the linear actuator via a Bluetooth module. Below are the electrical schematic, the system diagram explaining the electronic operation and transmission of the kart, as well as the block definition and architecture diagrams.",
        ["Portfolio.BacSti2d.Software.Title"] = "Control application",
        ["Portfolio.BacSti2d.Software.Description"] = "To drive the kart, I developed a mobile application using Unity. The interface allows sending steering and speed commands via Bluetooth to the Arduino mounted on the kart. I used Unity because I already knew how to build an application with it, and it was, in my opinion, the simplest solution to handle the phone’s motion detection.",
        ["Portfolio.BacSti2d.Videos.Title"] = "Demonstrations",
        ["Portfolio.BacSti2d.Videos.Description"] = "Here are videos showing how it works: the remote movement of the kart, and the actuator tests for steering.",
        ["Portfolio.BacSti2d.Management.Title"] = "Project management (Gantt)",
        ["Portfolio.BacSti2d.Management.Description"] = "The project took place over several months. We used a Gantt chart to plan and distribute tasks within our group of three.",
